  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some patchy fog/low stratus is once again\npossible late tonight into Friday, mainly across southeast VA \nand northeast NC. Otherwise, dry, seasonable weather pattern is\nlooking likely for Halloween weekend and into early next week. \n\nLatest weather analysis shows 1028+ mb high pressure offshore \nof the northeast coast, ridging back across the lower mid-\nAtlantic. Low pressure continues to slide across S QC, with the\nassociated cold front over the eastern Great Lakes, extending\nback into the Central Plains. Locally, early morning stratus and\nfog have cleared most of the area, though some lingering strato\ncu continues to slowly erode over southside Hampton Roads into\nnortheast NC. Temperatures have warmed well into the 60s to\nlower 70s over much of the region, with even a few mid 70s\nacross south central VA and northeast NC. Expect low clouds \nre-develop late tonight into early Friday, mainly across the \nsouth/southeastern portion of the area around sunrise Friday\nmorning, quickly eroding by mid-morning.\n\nThe cold front to the north drops into the region tomorrow\nafternoon. However, its initial push of cold air advection will\nbe delayed until later tomorrow evening. Given plenty of\nsunshine and dry W-NW flow, expect one more warm day with \nforecast highs into the upper 70s and low 80s. These temperatures\nin tandem with Td values falling into the upper 40s and low 50s\nwill yield minimum RH values of 35-40% in the afternoon across \ninterior VA and NE NC. Cool air advection arrives tomorrow\nnight, and will cool temperatures back to near/just above \nclimatological normal for the weekend. Halloween looks dry and \npleasant, with highs in the 60s and increasing clouds Saturday \nevening for the trick-or-treaters and temperatures mainly in the\n50s to near 60. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances have trended down for early next\nweek. A better chance for rain looks to develop for the latter\nhalf of next week. \n\nThe chance for measurable rainfall continues to decrease for \nSunday night into Monday. Model guidance has trended weaker with\nan upper trough currently over the Pacific Northwest, and \nprobabilistic guidance has accordingly lowered QPF with this \ninitial early-week shortwave. While a weak QPF signal lingers\nwith the 12z GFS across southern Virginia and northern North \nCarolina on Monday, precipitation will have to overcome a dry \nantecedent airmass reinforced by building surface and mid/upper-\nlevel ridging. Most of the rest of the guidance remains in good\nagreement that this period remains dry. A better signal for \nmeasurable rainfall arrives with the next shortwave later in \nthe week. However, forecast confidence in this potential welcome\nrainfall remains understandably low given the timing.",
